{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfba5x76m7wMgn7MaWroq", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Flags here are simple: you declare them in a TOML file, and code reads them through one helper.\n\n**1. Declare the flag in `flags.toml`** at the repo root. Names are kebab-case:\n\n```toml\n[flags]\ninvoice-pdf-v2 = false\nnew-checkout-flow = false\n```\n\n**2. Check it in code with `flags.is_on()`** from `src/flags.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom src import flags  # adjust to however the module imports flags\n\nif flags.is_on(\"new-checkout-flow\"):\n    return new_checkout(...)\nreturn legacy_checkout(...)\n```\n\nThings to know, from `src/flags.py`:\n- **Flags load once, at import time.** `flags.toml` is parsed when the module loads (`src/flags.py:4`), so changing a value needs a process restart. There's no runtime toggle, per-user targeting or environment override.\n- **Unknown names count as off.** `is_on()` returns `False` for any name not in the file (`src/flags.py:8`). A typo in the flag name won't raise an error; it just leaves the feature off. That's why the convention is to declare every flag in `flags.toml`.\n- **`flags.all_flags()`** returns a copy of all flag values, which helps with debugging or showing the current state.\n\nThe project's memory store records the same convention: read flags only through `flags.is_on(\"name\")`, use kebab-case names, and declare them in `flags.toml`. That matches what the code does. If you need per-user rollout or runtime toggling for the checkout flow, `src/flags.py` would need extending; right now flags are global on/off only."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 426, "cache_read_input_tokens": 21613, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 426}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "infer